# Kavis — earlyoom settings (item 8)
#
# earlyoom kills the hungriest process BEFORE RAM runs out and prevents
# the system from locking up (the kernel's own OOM acts far too late).
#
# --avoid is REQUIRED (item 59 scan: "kills the wrong process" is
# earlyoom's best-known open complaint): if the processes that keep the
# session up are killed, the user is left on a black screen — contrary
# to Kavis's "safe return to the desktop" principle. Hungry but harmless
# victims such as browser processes are moved to the front with --prefer.
#
# NOTE — NO QUOTES AND NO SPACES: the service file expands $EARLYOOM_ARGS
# WITHOUT a shell, with systemd's own whitespace splitting; systemd does
# not understand quotes. So the regexes must be single words — a space in
# a process name is written as [[:space:]] ("Isolated Web Co" = Firefox's
# comm name truncated to 15 characters).
#
# -r 3600: a memory report once an hour (so the log does not fill up);
# memory/swap thresholds are the defaults (10%/5%).
EARLYOOM_ARGS=-r 3600 --avoid (^|/)(systemd|systemd-logind|dbus-daemon|Xorg|lightdm|openbox|picom|kavis-panel)$ --prefer (^|/)(firefox|firefox-esr|chromium|Web[[:space:]]Content|Isolated[[:space:]]Web[[:space:]]Co)$
